<div class="bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100">
  <.marketing_nav current_user={@current_user} current={:docs} />

  <section class="py-12 sm:py-16">
    <.docs_layout
      current="troubleshooting"
      updated="August 4, 2026"
      toc={[
        {"runner", "The runner will not connect"},
        {"packs", "A pack or an action is missing"},
        {"dispatch", "An action will not dispatch"},
        {"runs", "A run failed, timed out, or looks stuck"},
        {"mcp", "The LLM client or the bridge"},
        {"identity", "Sign-in and directory sync"},
        {"audit", "The audit poller"},
        {"evidence", "Collect this before you report it"},
        {"escalate", "Where to send it"}
      ]}
    >
      <.docs_header current="troubleshooting">
        <:dek>
          Start with what you can see. Run the smallest check that separates the possible causes.
          Then open the page that owns the fix. If you need help, collect a report that already
          contains the required evidence.
        </:dek>
      </.docs_header>

      <.docs_prerequisites>
        <:item>
          The console shows run, runner, and pack state. Any role can read runs. An admin or owner
          is required to change trust, keys, or policy.
        </:item>
        <:item>
          A shell on the affected host for the runner-side checks, with
          <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">sudo</code>
          — the config, token, and packs are root-owned.
        </:item>
        <:item>
          The UTC window the problem started in. Every check below narrows faster with one.
        </:item>
      </.docs_prerequisites>

      <p class="mt-6 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        This page routes to the reference. It does not repeat it. You are done when the owner page
        explains the symptom and its check passes. If the check still fails, each symptom tells you
        when to escalate. The last two sections list exactly what to send.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="runner">The runner will not connect</.docs_h2>

      <.docs_h3 id="never-appears">It never appears in the fleet</.docs_h3>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The installer finished, but the runner is not in the Runners list at all. It never
        completed its first registration, so there is no identity to show.
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-5 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">First, read the host log.</strong>
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">
              sudo journalctl -u emisar -n 200
            </code>
            names the failure. A <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">401</code>
            from registration means the enrollment key was spent, expired, out of uses, or revoked
            — mint a fresh one and reinstall with it. A
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">400 invalid_external_id</code>
            means the host offered no usable identity. The runner uses its configured <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">runner.id</code>.
            If none is set, it uses its hostname. The value must contain at least one nonblank
            character and no more than 255 characters.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">A
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">409 runner_name_taken</code>
            is a name conflict, not a credential problem.</strong>
            Another runner in this account already holds that name, online or not. A runner is
            named after the host it registered from, and that name is fixed at registration —
            nothing renames a runner afterwards, and
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">runner.id</code>
            sets the identity, not the name. A rebuilt host, a restored image, and two hosts
            sharing a hostname all land here. Delete the existing runner under
            <.link
              navigate={~p"/app/runners"}
              class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
            >Runners</.link>
            to free the name, or give this host a different hostname. Deleting is terminal —
            <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/runner-fleet"}
              class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
            >Manage the runner fleet</.link>
            covers the difference from disabling.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">A
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">402 runner_limit_exceeded</code>
            is the plan's runner cap.</strong>
            The response names the plan and its limit; the free plan allows three. Delete a runner
            the account no longer uses, or move to a plan with more —
            <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/billing"}
              class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
            >Billing and plans</.link>
            has the per-plan numbers.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Then check egress.</strong>
            A connect that hangs rather than failing is a blocked path, not a bad credential. The
            runner needs outbound TLS to your emisar origin and nothing inbound — the exact hosts
            and ports are in <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/network-requirements"}
              class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
            >Network requirements</.link>.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Escalate</strong>
            when a freshly minted, unused key still returns <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">401</code>, or registration
            returns a status the log cannot explain. Enrollment keys and their caps are owned by <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/runner-fleet"}
              class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
            >Manage the runner fleet</.link>.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <.docs_h3 id="offline">It is offline or keeps reconnecting</.docs_h3>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The runner registered once and is now offline. Reconnecting on its own is normal. The
        runner connects again with bounded backoff and advertises its packs and actions. An action
        already running continues through that gap.
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-5 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">First, look at the host.</strong>
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">systemctl status emisar</code>
            shows whether the process is running.
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">sudo emisar doctor</code>
            checks config, credentials, packs, and action binaries. It also checks service state
            and reachability without opening a cloud session.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Then check the identity, not the host.</strong>
            A disabled runner gets a
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">403</code>
            and retries with its existing token. Enabling it restores the host without shell
            access. A disabled account behaves the same way for every runner in it. A deleted
            runner gets a terminal <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">401</code>.
            It discards its cached token and stops retrying. That host must enroll again.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Escalate</strong>
            when the service is up, the runner is enabled,
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">doctor</code>
            passes including reachability, and the fleet still shows it offline across several
            backoff cycles.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <.docs_h3 id="crash-loop">The service failed or is crash-looping</.docs_h3>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        systemd reports <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">failed</code>. After five starts in
        five minutes, it stops retrying on purpose. This prevents a revoked credential from
        hammering the control plane.
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-5 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">First, read the exit, then re-arm the unit.</strong>
            Fix what the log names, then <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">
              sudo systemctl reset-failed emisar &amp;&amp; sudo systemctl start emisar
            </code>. Starting without resetting the failure counter does nothing.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Then check for a second process.</strong>
            A runner locks its data directory at boot. Only one process can own that dispatch
            and replay state at a time. A stray manual
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">emisar connect</code>
            beside the service is the usual cause.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Escalate</strong>
            when <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">doctor</code>
            reports every check green and the service still exits. Install layout, the service
            unit, and the paths it writes are owned by <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/host-install"}
              class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
            >Install on a host</.link>.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <.docs_h2 id="packs">A pack or an action is missing</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Four different states look the same from the catalog — the action you wanted is not
        offered. They separate on the host and on the Packs page.
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-5 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">The pack did not load.</strong>
            The runner reports the pack as degraded and includes the loader's reason. It does not
            drop the pack silently. The console can then name the failed pack and host. Reproduce
            the problem locally with <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">
              sudo emisar pack validate ./pack
            </code>. Duplicate action IDs, a script escaping the pack root, and unexpected symlinks all
            fail closed — set
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">allow_symlinks</code>
            when a pack needs them. The output names the file and rule.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">The pack is installed but pending.</strong>
            Installing bytes does not trust them. Review the exact version and content hash in <a
              href={~p"/app/packs"}
              class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
            >Packs</a>. Then trust it deliberately.
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">sudo emisar pack list</code>
            shows what the host advertises.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">The hash drifted.</strong>
            Trust binds one exact content hash, so bytes that changed under the same version block
            dispatch until someone reviews them. That is the guard working — treat it as a change
            you did not expect until you can explain it.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">One action is missing from an otherwise healthy pack.</strong>
            A runner advertises whether each action's program resolves on its <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">PATH</code>.
            If it does not, the action remains advertised but disappears from the executable
            targets. Install the missing binary or fix the service's <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">PATH</code>.
            The runner refreshes on its own. Local admission rules can also hide actions by ID or
            risk ceiling. A rule in the host's config always wins.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Escalate</strong>
            when the host hash matches the trusted hash and dispatch still refuses.
            <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/pack-updates"}
              class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
            >Roll out and roll back packs</.link>
            covers rollout, trust, and rollback.
            <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/action-packs"}
              class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
            >Pack reference</.link>
            contains the YAML contract.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <.docs_h2 id="dispatch">An action will not dispatch</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Dispatch from
        <a
          href={~p"/app/runners"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Runners</a>
        first, even if the agent is what failed. The run row tells you
        which gate stopped it, and that is faster than reading an error twice removed.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-5 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-zinc-800">
        <table class="w-full min-w-[720px] text-left text-sm">
          <thead class="bg-zinc-900/60 text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-zinc-400">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3">What you see</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3">What stopped it</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3">Where it is fixed</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-zinc-900 text-zinc-300">
            <%= for {seen, cause, {owner_path, owner}} <- [
              {"The runner or the action is not offered at all",
               "Runner scope. A member or key sees only the hosts it was granted. An out-of-scope runner is hidden instead of refused.",
               {~p"/docs/teams-and-access", "Teams & access"}},
              {"Dispatch is refused before a run appears",
               "Pack trust. A pending or drifted content hash is not dispatchable, by design.",
               {~p"/docs/pack-updates", "Roll out and roll back packs"}},
              {"The run lands Denied",
               "Policy. The matched rule and its reason are recorded on the run.",
               {~p"/docs/policies-and-approvals", "Policies & approvals"}},
              {"The run sits at Pending approval",
               "Policy asked for a person. It waits for an approver. A denial cancels it.",
               {~p"/docs/policies-and-approvals", "Policies & approvals"}},
              {"The run lands Refused",
               "The runner rejected it on a pre-execution check — a pack-hash mismatch, a signature problem, or local admission. Nothing executed.",
               {~p"/docs/signed-dispatch", "Signed dispatch"}},
              {"The run lands Error without reaching the host",
               "The runner was offline, disabled, or removed, or never acknowledged the send before the dispatch timeout.",
               {~p"/docs/runner-fleet", "Runner fleet"}}
            ] do %>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top font-semibold text-zinc-100">{seen}</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top leading-6">{cause}</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top leading-6">
                  <.link navigate={owner_path} class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200">
                    {owner}
                  </.link>
                </td>
              </tr>
            <% end %>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Over MCP, these conditions deliberately share one response. Every stale or hidden target
        condition returns
        <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">target_contract_changed</code>
        with the exact refresh call. This prevents a stale caller from learning which hidden fact
        changed.
        Follow that refresh once, then retry once.
        <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">signature_required</code>
        names the runners that enforce client signing.
        <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">not_allowed</code>
        means the current scope does not permit the request. Do not use it as an invitation to
        probe.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Escalate</strong>
        when all these conditions hold. Policy allows the action. Its trusted hash is current. The
        runner is online and in scope. Dispatch still refuses, and the run shows no matched rule.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="runs">A run failed, timed out, or looks stuck</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Read the status first. The
        <.link navigate={~p"/docs/runs"} class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200">Runs</.link>
        page lists every status. Three are easy to confuse.
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Failed</strong>
        means the action ran and exited with a nonzero status. This is a real result to read, not a
        gate. <strong class="text-zinc-200">Error</strong>
        means the action did not complete. The runner went offline, was disabled or removed, or never
        acknowledged the send. <strong class="text-zinc-200">Timed out</strong>
        means the action ran past its declared limit and the runner stopped it.
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-5 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">A queued run is usually not stuck.</strong>
            It waits for its runner and is delivered as soon as that runner reconnects. Only when
            the host is still gone after a short grace window does the sweep end it as
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Error</strong>
            rather than leave it queued indefinitely.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">A run that ends with
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">execution_outcome_unknown</code>
            can have completed its work.</strong>
            The runner reports this when its process or host dies after execution starts. The
            terminal result was not yet durable. emisar never runs that dispatch again
            automatically. Do not retry until you check the target system. emisar does not control
            the side effect.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Output stopped but the run is still running.</strong>
            Output is bounded per action and truncation is reported, so a quiet run is more often a
            quiet command than a lost stream. Check the host with <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">sudo emisar events tail -f</code>,
            which shows the local journal live.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Escalate</strong>
            when the same dispatch reports
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">execution_outcome_unknown</code>
            repeatedly, or a run stays <strong class="text-zinc-200">Running</strong>
            with no progress while its runner is connected and idle.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <.docs_h2 id="mcp">The LLM client or the bridge</.docs_h2>
      <ul class="mt-5 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Every call returns 401.</strong>
            The key expired, was revoked, or a rotation successor retired it. An expired key may
            already have a live successor the bridge minted — look before you mint another. A
            revoked one is terminal. If the client never worked at all, check which token was
            copied: audit-export tokens share the
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">emk-</code>
            prefix and the MCP endpoint refuses them as the wrong kind. Mint or rotate a key in
            <a
              href={~p"/app/agents"}
              class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
            >AI agents</a>
            and put the new secret in the client config.
            <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/agents-and-keys"}
              class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
            >Manage agents & keys</.link>
            covers scope and expiry.
            <.link navigate={~p"/docs/credentials"} class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200">Rotate and revoke credentials</.link>
            covers the cutover.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">The bridge is outdated or unsupported.</strong>
            The Agents page shows the version last reported by each key.
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">emisar-mcp --version</code>
            confirms the installed version. A running client keeps the binary it already loaded.
            Restart the client after installation. See <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/bridge-upgrades"}
              class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
            >Upgrade the MCP bridge</.link>.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">A tool call comes back with an error code.</strong>
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">invalid_args</code>
            returns the exact paths to correct, so fix those rather than retrying the same call.
            The complete code list, with the one action each implies, is in <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/mcp-reference"}
              class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
            >MCP CLI &amp; reference</.link>.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">The response was lost after a mutation.</strong>
            Recover by operation ID with <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">get_operation</code>.
            Use the same credential lineage that made the call. Never repeat the mutation. A
            cancelled call, closed client, or dropped bridge does not undo work the control plane
            already admitted. Cancellation stops the bridge's wait. Closing the client's input
            lets requests already in flight finish.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Escalate</strong>
            when the same tool returns a transport error on every attempt. Also escalate when a
            recorded operation ID is missing under the credential that created it.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <.docs_h2 id="identity">Sign-in and directory sync</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Both surfaces keep their own symptom tables, because the fix is nearly always at the
        provider. Two cases are worth knowing before you open them.
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-5 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">SSO sign-in fails, or members loop back to the provider.</strong>
            A discovery failure means the issuer URL is wrong. A rejected callback means the
            redirect URI or client secret is wrong. An accepted login that emisar refuses means
            the ID token lacks the configured identifier or a verified email. If Require SSO is
            on, use an open owner session to turn it off when all real sign-ins fail.
            <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/sso#troubleshooting"}
              class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
            >Single sign-on</.link>
            contains the full table.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Provisioning or group push stopped working.</strong>
            A rotated SCIM bearer invalidates the old value immediately, with no overlap — the
            connector fails until you paste the new one in. A group mapping that does nothing is
            almost always keyed on a display name instead of the directory's <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">externalId</code>. The full
            table is in <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/scim#troubleshooting"}
              class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
            >Directory sync</.link>.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Escalate</strong>
            when the provider reports success, the identifier claims match, and emisar still
            refuses the identity. Also escalate when Require SSO is on and no owner session
            remains.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <.docs_h2 id="audit">The audit poller</.docs_h2>
      <ul class="mt-5 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">It stopped advancing.</strong>
            An empty page sets neither
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">X-Next-Cursor</code>
            nor <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">Link</code>. The collector keeps
            its last cursor. This means it is caught up, not stuck. Only a full page includes <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">Link: rel="next"</code>.
            That header means more data can be available now.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Events repeat, or look missing.</strong>
            The export uses a forward keyset read. Save the returned cursor and resume strictly
            after it. A collector that restarts from a timestamp rereads events. Process or hand
            off the page in an idempotent way. Then save its returned cursor. A crash between those
            steps can repeat the page. It cannot skip unprocessed events. Expect duplicate events
            instead of assuming exactly-once delivery.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">It gets 429, 401, or 403.</strong>
            The endpoint allows 60 requests per minute for each token. Poll on an interval, not in
            a loop. It returns <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">429</code>
            above that rate. A <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">401</code>
            means the bearer is unknown or revoked. A
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">403</code>
            means authentication succeeded, but this token lacks access. Causes include using an
            agent key instead of an audit-export token. Other causes are a plan without export or a
            role without audit access. The two credential types never substitute for each other.
            The poller must keep its cursor and retry. A failed request is not an empty page.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Escalate</strong>
            when a page returns events you can see in the console with a cursor that does not
            advance past them. The export contract is owned by <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/audit-and-siem"}
              class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
            >Audit &amp; SIEM</.link>.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <.docs_h2 id="evidence">Collect this before you report it</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Reports with this evidence usually need one response. Reports without it can cost a day of
        questions.
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-5 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Versions of every component involved</strong>
            — the runner
            (<code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">emisar --version</code>), the
            bridge
            (<code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">emisar-mcp --version</code>),
            and the LLM client.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">The UTC time window</strong>
            in which it happened, not "this morning".
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Safe labels, not secrets</strong>
            — the runner name and group, and the account name. Never the enrollment key, the runner
            token, an API key, or a signing key.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">The identifiers</strong>
            — the run id, the operation id, and the request id where you have them. They are how we
            find the same event you are looking at.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">
              <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">sudo emisar doctor</code>
              output
            </strong>
            from the affected host, whole rather than the one line you think matters.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">A bounded slice of the logs</strong>
            around the window —
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">
              sudo journalctl -u emisar -n 200
            </code>
            and <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">
              sudo emisar events tail --lines 100
            </code>. A whole journal is harder to read, not more complete.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">The exact error text</strong>
            and whether it reproduces — every time, sometimes, or once.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <.docs_callout kind={:warn} title="Redact before you send.">
        <p>
          Never paste a raw token, key, or certificate into a support request, ticket, chat message,
          or run reason. The runner redacts action output before it leaves the host. Provider output
          copied by hand has not passed through that redaction. Read it before attaching it. Mask
          anything that looks like a credential. If a secret is already exposed, treat it as an
          incident, not a support request. Go to <.link
            navigate={~p"/docs/security-incidents"}
            class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
          >Security incidents</.link>.
        </p>
      </.docs_callout>

      <.docs_h2 id="escalate">Where to send it</.docs_h2>
      <ul class="mt-5 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">A product problem</strong>
            goes to
            <.link navigate={~p"/support"} class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200">Support</.link>
            with the evidence above. This includes anything broken, unclear, or slower than
            expected.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">A suspected compromise or a vulnerability</strong>
            goes to <a
              href={EmisarWeb.MailTo.security()}
              class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
            >security@emisar.dev</a>. Examples include a leaked credential, an unexplained action,
            or an unexpected content hash.
            <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/security-incidents"}
              class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
            >Security incidents</.link>
            lists the containment steps. Contain first. Send the report within the hour.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </.docs_layout>
  </section>

  <.marketing_footer />
</div>
